WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:04.800
Hello, and welcome back to another video
with ItsDrDan, and today we're going to be

00:00:04.810 --> 00:00:07.790
learning how to read a chemical formula.

00:00:08.319 --> 00:00:11.399
More specifically, we're going to
learn how to count the number of

00:00:11.540 --> 00:00:15.930
atoms you have within the different
compounds you find in these formulas.

00:00:16.765 --> 00:00:18.615
So, what is this all about?

00:00:18.695 --> 00:00:20.055
What do we know so far?

00:00:20.865 --> 00:00:24.695
Well, the first thing is that we know
is that atoms make up all matter.

00:00:25.145 --> 00:00:29.565
And that atoms are made up of different
subatomic particles, which is leading

00:00:29.574 --> 00:00:34.445
to them bonding together because they
want and desire all these different

00:00:34.454 --> 00:00:38.925
numbers of electrons in order to
try to achieve the octet rule.

00:00:39.720 --> 00:00:45.610
However, uh, most of the, most matter that
we see do not really exist just as atoms.

00:00:45.650 --> 00:00:49.410
As we're starting to realize,
most of matter that you see around

00:00:49.410 --> 00:00:51.219
you is in the form of compounds.

00:00:51.589 --> 00:00:53.869
Uh, whether it's maybe like the
different materials that make up your

00:00:53.870 --> 00:00:58.380
phone or the screen on it, the, your,
maybe your laptop keys, the walls of

00:00:58.380 --> 00:01:02.120
your home, uh, the, maybe the desk
that you're sitting at your bed, all

00:01:02.120 --> 00:01:04.040
these things are made up of compounds.

00:01:04.450 --> 00:01:07.380
This is the majority of things
that exist in our world.

00:01:07.965 --> 00:01:11.685
Uh, so what we're gonna try to
do is learn how we can count them

00:01:11.685 --> 00:01:16.175
here and try to understand basic
concepts in this fundamental video.

00:01:16.744 --> 00:01:17.865
Let's take a look.

00:01:27.445 --> 00:01:31.925
The first thing about chemical formulas is
that they can have the following things.

00:01:32.404 --> 00:01:35.804
So the first one is that they will
have the element symbols that you

00:01:35.804 --> 00:01:37.764
can find on your periodic table.

00:01:38.354 --> 00:01:41.685
Now, this is one of the reasons it's
very important that you start, you

00:01:41.765 --> 00:01:43.615
are memorizing your periodic table.

00:01:43.655 --> 00:01:46.815
It makes it easier to do your
general chemistry course.

00:01:47.324 --> 00:01:52.104
Um, even some classes don't even
give you the complete information.

00:01:52.495 --> 00:01:56.374
It fully depends on what, who your
teacher is, what they're giving you.

00:01:56.724 --> 00:01:58.095
Need to know those symbols.

00:01:58.595 --> 00:02:02.244
As a general rule of thumb for me and
my students, I recommend the first

00:02:02.244 --> 00:02:06.184
20, know the symbols, know your names,
because they are some of the most

00:02:06.184 --> 00:02:07.835
common that we have in our world.

00:02:08.695 --> 00:02:11.695
Um, after that, we're going
to be using subscripts.

00:02:12.075 --> 00:02:14.185
So what exactly is a subscript?

00:02:14.475 --> 00:02:21.305
So if I have, let's say, water right
here, H2O, a subscript is a number

00:02:21.325 --> 00:02:24.894
that's written as like a lower font.

00:02:25.445 --> 00:02:29.114
Um, so this is what we
refer to as a subscript.

00:02:29.684 --> 00:02:35.575
Uh, so what subscripts are for
are telling you the number of

00:02:35.695 --> 00:02:38.315
atoms that are in this molecule.

00:02:38.765 --> 00:02:43.094
So for this one, this is letting
you know that you have two

00:02:43.104 --> 00:02:46.065
hydrogen atoms that are here.

00:02:46.454 --> 00:02:48.695
Okay, so we have two hydrogens.

00:02:49.454 --> 00:02:54.844
Um, so that is a very important piece of
information for when we say we have water.

00:02:54.855 --> 00:02:54.914
Thank you.

00:02:55.254 --> 00:02:55.545
here.

00:02:56.184 --> 00:03:00.445
Sometimes you'll also see the
use of parentheses as well.

00:03:00.924 --> 00:03:05.204
Now we'll talk about them more in
detail soon, but they're usually used

00:03:05.225 --> 00:03:12.054
to group certain, uh, polyatomics
or formula units together.

00:03:12.104 --> 00:03:17.405
So that way you are, you are counting
and balancing your charges correctly.

00:03:17.405 --> 00:03:17.925
Okay.

00:03:17.925 --> 00:03:23.204
It's very important that when
something's in parentheses, They stay

00:03:23.554 --> 00:03:26.474
together, they belong together, okay?

00:03:26.795 --> 00:03:31.344
Just letting you know how many units
of that particular molecule you have.

00:03:31.844 --> 00:03:36.184
Okay, so for this particular one, you
can see that you have two hydrogens,

00:03:36.694 --> 00:03:39.865
and then we have a single oxygen in H2O.

00:03:40.475 --> 00:03:44.045
So what about the subscript
that is over here for oxygen?

00:03:44.424 --> 00:03:46.644
Notice that it's currently vacant.

00:03:46.935 --> 00:03:51.215
So what that means is
that there is a one here.

00:03:51.605 --> 00:03:54.565
So it is telling you that
there's only one element.

00:03:54.605 --> 00:03:56.615
Generally, we leave it blank.

00:03:56.914 --> 00:04:00.675
But depending on how you are being
graded on different assessments, you

00:04:00.675 --> 00:04:02.195
might have to put the number one there.

00:04:02.715 --> 00:04:05.405
Um, especially if it's something
that's more of an auto graded response.

00:04:05.635 --> 00:04:07.905
But that means there's one single oxygen.

00:04:08.415 --> 00:04:10.495
So let's write that down.

00:04:11.135 --> 00:04:17.005
Alright, let's go on to our next part, so
that way we can learn a little bit more

00:04:17.014 --> 00:04:18.615
of these different chemical formulas.

00:04:18.954 --> 00:04:23.415
Alright, so I have magnesium
hydroxide here written for us, and

00:04:23.415 --> 00:04:28.335
we are going to go through what the
general rule is for parentheses.

00:04:28.745 --> 00:04:31.475
So how exactly do they work?

00:04:31.745 --> 00:04:35.245
Well, what the first thing we are
looking for is our subscripts.

00:04:35.675 --> 00:04:40.385
One thing you can notice right away, there
is no subscript written next to magnesium.

00:04:40.725 --> 00:04:42.984
So what can we exactly say about that?

00:04:43.335 --> 00:04:48.305
Is that Mg, there is one
atom of Mg here, right?

00:04:48.305 --> 00:04:49.735
So one atom of magnesium.

00:04:50.229 --> 00:04:52.190
Now, what about the parentheses here?

00:04:52.200 --> 00:04:54.109
How does this work?

00:04:54.560 --> 00:05:01.580
Well, the general idea here is that this 2
is going to be distributed to each of the

00:05:01.609 --> 00:05:09.284
individual elements within, meaning it's
multiplying each one of those elements.

00:05:09.624 --> 00:05:13.534
So being that each one of these
has a little box next to them,

00:05:13.905 --> 00:05:15.525
remember, they're just one.

00:05:15.574 --> 00:05:20.604
So in a sense, the way that this
works is if I have oxygen here, you

00:05:20.605 --> 00:05:26.255
are essentially taking one times, uh,
two in this case, which is telling

00:05:26.255 --> 00:05:28.815
you, hey, you have two oxygen atoms.

00:05:29.830 --> 00:05:32.630
And that's the same thing
for hydrogen, right?

00:05:32.860 --> 00:05:37.630
We can, we do two times one and we
can see that we have two hydrogen

00:05:37.670 --> 00:05:40.909
atoms within magnesium hydroxide.

00:05:41.430 --> 00:05:46.740
So when we're trying to understand
this general rule here, when you're

00:05:46.749 --> 00:05:51.240
counting the number of atoms, first
you got to count how parentheses.

00:05:52.530 --> 00:05:58.293
So as you saw with oxygen and hydrogen
above, I counted one within each of them.

00:05:58.293 --> 00:06:03.899
Now most of the time when you have
parentheses, you generally have a number

00:06:04.169 --> 00:06:07.309
outside, meaning an additional subscript.

00:06:07.730 --> 00:06:11.184
So this is going to be used
to multiply what's within.

00:06:11.744 --> 00:06:13.385
So what do I mean by that?

00:06:13.405 --> 00:06:19.104
Let's just do a very, very hypothetical
example like the one that is here.

00:06:19.725 --> 00:06:21.175
So what are we going to do?

00:06:21.175 --> 00:06:26.095
Let's count these numbers and letters
for A, B, and C, these fake elements.

00:06:26.394 --> 00:06:27.355
How would it work?

00:06:27.845 --> 00:06:30.885
The first thing I want to do is let's
just count the number of A elements.

00:06:31.425 --> 00:06:35.185
And what you can see is, is
that, oh, we got a W here.

00:06:35.375 --> 00:06:37.544
It's not in parentheses.

00:06:37.545 --> 00:06:44.364
So what that tells you is that A is
equal to, uh, W amount of atoms, right?

00:06:44.374 --> 00:06:45.664
So W atoms.

00:06:46.384 --> 00:06:47.834
What about B?

00:06:47.905 --> 00:06:54.344
Okay, so B has the subscript of
X next to it, but notice that

00:06:54.344 --> 00:06:57.645
it's inside of these parentheses.

00:06:58.360 --> 00:07:00.410
So how does that exactly work?

00:07:00.830 --> 00:07:04.050
The is going to distribute itself.

00:07:04.545 --> 00:07:06.125
to both of these species.

00:07:06.655 --> 00:07:12.105
Now, when we are writing
formulas, you do not carry this

00:07:12.105 --> 00:07:13.455
out like a normal math problem.

00:07:13.964 --> 00:07:18.804
We're just using this to count the number
of atoms, so you know how many there are.

00:07:19.115 --> 00:07:21.555
We leave it in its formula, okay?

00:07:21.564 --> 00:07:25.995
So it's there, it's in that format
for a reason, because it's letting you

00:07:25.995 --> 00:07:27.925
know the different units that exist.

00:07:28.305 --> 00:07:34.515
But when we're counting atoms, all's
we're doing is taking x times z, and

00:07:34.515 --> 00:07:38.735
that's telling you however many atoms
you have, okay, so that's the number.

00:07:39.385 --> 00:07:44.455
Now when it comes to c, same idea,
right, we're gonna take y times z,

00:07:44.515 --> 00:07:46.929
and you'll have that many atoms.

00:07:47.640 --> 00:07:50.080
Let's take a look at a real
one so that maybe it'll help

00:07:50.180 --> 00:07:51.860
make a little bit more sense.

00:07:52.050 --> 00:07:52.970
Let's take a look.

00:07:53.190 --> 00:07:56.620
Okay, so in this example, we are
going to be counting how many of

00:07:56.630 --> 00:07:59.589
each atom exist within a formula.

00:07:59.640 --> 00:08:03.240
So this is very similar to the
alphabetical one we had earlier.

00:08:03.699 --> 00:08:05.380
So what are we going to be doing first?

00:08:05.390 --> 00:08:08.289
Well, we got to realize we have a
bunch of different subscripts here.

00:08:08.599 --> 00:08:09.930
So let's first start with iron.

00:08:09.979 --> 00:08:12.070
We can see iron's
outside of the subscript.

00:08:12.260 --> 00:08:13.580
There's nothing written there.

00:08:13.914 --> 00:08:18.565
So we can automatically assume
we have one atom of iron.

00:08:19.285 --> 00:08:21.045
Now, what else do we have?

00:08:21.095 --> 00:08:22.335
Well, let's take a look.

00:08:22.335 --> 00:08:25.155
So if we look at nitrogen
next, we see that there's a,

00:08:25.215 --> 00:08:26.365
there's nothing written there.

00:08:26.375 --> 00:08:27.695
So once again, it's a one.

00:08:28.274 --> 00:08:29.995
So how is that going to work?

00:08:30.015 --> 00:08:32.754
Well, we have a two outside of it.

00:08:33.510 --> 00:08:38.690
So what we will do here is we'll
simply take that 2, times that by

00:08:38.700 --> 00:08:45.390
1, and we have a total of 2 atoms
of nitrogen within this compound.

00:08:46.069 --> 00:08:50.810
Now with, uh, with oxygen,
we have a 3 inside.

00:08:51.360 --> 00:08:57.310
We're going to multiply that as well,
so 3 times 2, and that lets you know,

00:08:57.320 --> 00:09:01.170
hey, you have 6 atoms of oxygen.

00:09:01.930 --> 00:09:06.690
Now, just to kind of illustrate, what
does this, what does this represent?

00:09:07.079 --> 00:09:10.999
Remember, ionic compounds exist
in a lattice type of network.

00:09:11.489 --> 00:09:15.890
So what this is kind of letting you
know is that, hey, we have this Iron

00:09:15.890 --> 00:09:23.060
2 plus that's here, and there are
different nitrate units on each side.

00:09:23.070 --> 00:09:26.339
So it's kind of like we have,
let's say, an NO3 on one side.

00:09:27.125 --> 00:09:28.525
NO3 on the other.

00:09:29.265 --> 00:09:33.495
And they are being, they're
surrounding this iron and it's

00:09:33.505 --> 00:09:35.965
in a repeating framework, okay?

00:09:35.965 --> 00:09:37.665
So there's many of these in a row.

00:09:38.755 --> 00:09:40.585
And notice how I'm keeping NO3s together.

00:09:40.885 --> 00:09:41.715
That's the important part.

00:09:41.715 --> 00:09:43.604
The parentheses keep them together.

00:09:43.945 --> 00:09:46.235
You do not carry it out
like in math operation.

00:09:46.800 --> 00:09:50.680
Um, generally the way you will see these
is how it was originally presented.

00:09:51.150 --> 00:09:53.260
Let's take a look at our next one.

00:09:54.500 --> 00:10:00.310
So our next one here is essentially
we have calcium arsenate.

00:10:00.879 --> 00:10:03.640
So calcium arsenate is the same idea.

00:10:03.640 --> 00:10:07.690
So we're going to be counting all
these different species that we have.

00:10:08.280 --> 00:10:10.480
So we notice there's some subscripts here.

00:10:10.900 --> 00:10:12.750
So the first one was for calcium.

00:10:13.079 --> 00:10:16.030
We have three atoms of calcium.

00:10:17.155 --> 00:10:21.925
Now we have once again our parentheses,
we have a two outside of that.

00:10:21.925 --> 00:10:27.295
So we're going to be distributing it
to all the different species within.

00:10:28.035 --> 00:10:32.565
The first one is Arsenate, which
only has a one next to it, and

00:10:32.570 --> 00:10:34.815
we're going to multiply that by two.

00:10:35.925 --> 00:10:40.575
Oxygen has a four next to it, and
we're also gonna multiply that by two.

00:10:41.314 --> 00:10:43.895
And so we have two Arsenate.

00:10:44.564 --> 00:10:53.095
atoms and then eight oxygen
atoms in this compound.

00:10:54.035 --> 00:10:56.905
So this is the whole idea of when
we're looking at compounds and

00:10:56.905 --> 00:11:01.605
molecules, this is how we can count
them for chemical formulas and

00:11:01.615 --> 00:11:04.065
be able to read them correctly.

00:11:04.685 --> 00:11:06.075
Thank you so much for watching.

00:11:06.095 --> 00:11:08.645
I hope that this video helped you
understand chemical formulas a

00:11:08.645 --> 00:11:10.515
little bit better for the future.

00:11:10.865 --> 00:11:14.775
And please, if you have not already,
hit that like and subscribe button.

